TOPIC: 'SURD
1. DEFINITION OF SURD
A surd is a simplified form of a quantity that
contains a root \((\sqrt{ })\) in which no root appears
in the denominator.
\section*{Example:}
The surd of \(\frac{3}{\sqrt{2}} \quad \frac{3 \sqrt{2}}{2}\)
2. BASIC RULES FOR CLASSIFICATION OF SURD
- A surd must not have any surd in the denominator.
- If a surd is in the denominator, multiply both the
numerator and denominator by the surd or the quantity
that will remove the surd.
- The resulting denominator must be a rational number.
- After removing the surd, simplify the expression completey.
3. ADDITION OF SURDS
4. SUBTRACTION OF SURDS
Rule: Surds can only be added if
they have the same surd part
(same surd and same index).
Examples:
(i) \(2 \sqrt{3}+5 \sqrt{3}=(2+5) \sqrt{3}=7 \sqrt{3}\)
(ii) \(4 \sqrt{5}-3 \sqrt{5}+\sqrt{5}\)
\(=(4-3+1) \sqrt{5}\)
\(=2 \sqrt{5}\)
Note: Surds with different surd parts
cannot be added.
Example: \(2 \sqrt{2}+3 \sqrt{3}\) cannot be added.
\section*{4. SUBTRACTION OF SURDS}
Rule: Surds can only be subtracted if
they have the same surd part
(same surd and same index).
Examples:
(i) \(7 \sqrt{2}-3 \sqrt{2}=(7-3) \sqrt{2}=4 \sqrt{2}\)
(ii) \(5 \sqrt{6}-2 \sqrt{6}-\sqrt{6}\)
\(=(5-2-1) \sqrt{6}\)
\(=2 \sqrt{6}\)
